# Multi-scene showcase: Wave C, MASTER-PLAN parity row 26.
# One .f3d file may contain more than one `scene` block. Each block
# is a complete, self-contained scene -- its own name, size,
# background, camera, lights and objects -- and each one compiles
# and renders as its own output; nothing (objects, lights, camera)
# carries over from one block to the next. Below, the same kind of
# still life is staged twice, once small and dark, once larger on a
# light background with different objects, to make the separation
# obvious.
# Run: python3 tools/f3dc/f3dc.py run examples/multi-scene.f3d

# --- Scene 1 of 2 ---------------------------------------------------
# A small, dark scene: one lit cube and one sphere on a ground plane.
scene "multi-scene-one" size 320 240 background black
camera at 5 3 7 look 0 0 0
light directional dir -0.5 -1 -0.7 strength 1.2
plane ground_one gray at 0 -2 0 size 12
cube hero_cube red at -1.5 -1 0 size 2
sphere side_ball blue at 1.8 -1 0 radius 1
render

# --- Scene 2 of 2 ---------------------------------------------------
# A second `scene` statement starts a NEW scene block (row 26).
# This one has a different name, a different size (640x480, not
# 320x240), a different background (white, not black), its own
# camera and light, and completely different objects -- a pyramid
# and a ring where scene 1 had a cube and a sphere. It renders as
# its own separate output, exactly as if it were a file of its own.
scene "multi-scene-two" size 640 480 background white
camera at 6 4 9 look 0 0 0
light directional dir -0.5 -1 -0.7 strength 1.2
light ambient strength 0.2
plane ground_two gray at 0 -2 0 size 14
pyramid peak purple at -1.5 -1 0 size 2 height 2
torus ring orange at 2 0 0 radius 1
cone spike green at 4 -1 0 radius 1 height 2
render
